/* Reduction Ladder Atlas — histories for the third biology batch: immunology, physiology, ethology. */
const HIST_18 = {

immuno: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1890', who:'von Behring & Kitasato, Berlin', t:'Immunity in a bottle of serum',
   b:'Serum from an animal made resistant to diphtheria or tetanus toxin, injected into a naive animal, protects it. The demonstration converted immunity from a property of a body into a substance that could be drawn off, measured and sold — and within four years diphtheria antitoxin was in industrial production and the paediatric death rate in Berlin had fallen sharply. Two consequences for the theory. First, the object of study became the antibody molecule rather than the animal, which is why the field spent the next sixty years arguing about protein folding. Second, antitoxin could be titrated, so immunology acquired a number before it had a theory. Von Behring took the first Nobel in Medicine in 1901; Kitasato, a co-author of the founding paper, was not included.'},
  {y:'1897–1900', who:'Paul Ehrlich, Frankfurt', t:'A selection theory, proposed and then abandoned',
   b:'Ehrlich\'s side-chain theory says that cells carry pre-formed surface side-chains with which they normally take up nutrients; a toxin that happens to fit one blocks it; the cell compensates by overproducing that side-chain and sheds the surplus into the blood as antitoxin. This is a selection theory in everything but name — the specificity exists before the antigen and the antigen merely picks it out — and it is the structure Burnet would rediscover half a century later. Ehrlich also coined *horror autotoxicus*, the claim that an organism is constitutionally prevented from making antibody against itself, which fixed the problem of self-recognition as a problem before anyone could work on it. The theory was abandoned, by Ehrlich\'s own school as much as by anyone, because of what came next.'},
  {y:'1917–1936', who:'Karl Landsteiner, Vienna and New York', t:'The haptens, and why instruction became reasonable',
   b:'Landsteiner coupled small synthetic chemical groups — arsanilic acid, aminobenzene sulphonates, azo dyes — to carrier proteins and immunised rabbits. The animals made antibodies that distinguished *ortho* from *meta* from *para* substitution on a benzene ring. No vertebrate lineage had ever encountered these molecules; some did not exist before the dye industry. If specificity must pre-exist the antigen, the body would need a receptor for every compound a chemist can imagine, which in 1930 was not a proposition a serious person would defend. Landsteiner\'s work is what killed Ehrlich\'s selection theory, and it is important to see that it did so for a good reason. Instruction was not a failure of imagination; it was the conclusion a careful person drew from the best data available.'},
  {y:'1930–1940', who:'Breinl & Haurowitz; Linus Pauling, Caltech', t:'The template, given by the best chemist alive',
   b:'Breinl and Haurowitz proposed that antigen present during globulin synthesis directs the assembly of a complementary chain. Pauling, in 1940, replaced synthesis with folding: the polypeptide is the same in every antibody, and the antigen acts as a mould around which the ends of the chain fold into complementary shape. This is a good theory. It explains hapten specificity at no cost, it requires no new genetics, and it is stated in the structural vocabulary that was then transforming chemistry. Pauling also attempted to manufacture antibody in vitro by denaturing and refolding normal globulin in the presence of dyes, and reported partial success; the result was never reproduced, including in his own laboratory. Haurowitz remained a template theorist into the 1960s.'},
  {y:'1945–1953', who:'Owen; Burnet & Fenner; Medawar, Billingham & Brent', t:'Tolerance, arriving from cattle',
   b:'Ray Owen noticed in 1945 that dizygotic cattle twins, whose placentas fuse, carry each other\'s red cells for life without making antibody against them. Burnet and Fenner built this into the self-marker hypothesis in the 1949 edition of *The Production of Antibodies*: the embryo learns what is its own, and whatever is present during that window is thereafter exempt. Medawar, who had come to the problem from burn grafting during the war and believed graft rejection was immunological when most surgeons did not, saw that this was a prediction and tested it. The chain runs from an observation about freemartin cattle to a Nobel Prize shared with Burnet in 1960, and it is worth noting that the prediction was made by a theory — self-marking — that was itself wrong about the mechanism.'},
  {y:'1955–1957', who:'Niels Jerne, Copenhagen; David Talmage; Macfarlane Burnet, Melbourne', t:'Selection, restored — and moved from molecules to cells',
   b:'Jerne proposed that the body already contains a spontaneous diversity of globulin molecules; antigen picks the ones that fit, carries them into a phagocyte, and the phagocyte then makes more of that kind. The last step is a mechanism nobody could believe, and Jerne knew it. Talmage and then Burnet made the correction that mattered: the unit of selection is the cell, not the molecule, and a cell that has been selected proliferates. Burnet\'s paper is three pages in the *Australian Journal of Science*, with no mathematics and one figure. Its central move is to accept the absurdity Landsteiner had established — that the body carries specificities it can have no reason to carry — and pay for it with randomness and cell death.'},
  {y:'1959–1965', who:'Porter, Edelman; Dreyer & Bennett', t:'Two genes, one polypeptide — the heresy that was true',
   b:'Porter\'s papain digestion and Edelman\'s reduction of interchain disulphides gave the four-chain structure and, with it, the sequencing data showing that a light chain has a variable amino-terminal half and a constant carboxy-terminal half. This is an impossible object under the one-gene-one-polypeptide rule then universal, because the constant half is shared across thousands of antibodies with different variable halves. Dreyer and Bennett proposed in 1965 that two separate genes are joined somatically during lymphocyte development. The reception was poor — the paper was rejected before *PNAS* took it — and it made a flat, checkable prediction about DNA that nobody could yet test.'},
  {y:'1976–1990', who:'Hozumi & Tonegawa, Basel; Schatz, Oettinger & Baltimore, MIT', t:'The genome edits itself',
   b:'Tonegawa showed that immunoglobulin gene segments lie far apart in embryonic DNA and adjacent in an antibody-producing tumour — the first demonstration that a vertebrate somatic cell rearranges its own chromosomes as a matter of normal development. Nobel 1987. The recombinase responsible, RAG1 and RAG2, was isolated by Schatz, Oettinger and Baltimore in 1989–90 by transfecting fibroblasts with genomic fragments until they acquired the ability to join a test substrate. RAG turned out to be a domesticated transposase, so the adaptive immune system of every jawed vertebrate rests on a parasite that was captured about 500 million years ago and put to work generating the diversity Landsteiner had made look impossible.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'The haemolytic plaque assay: counting antibody-forming cells', who:'Niels Jerne & Albert Nordin, Pittsburgh', y:'1963', fig:'plaque',
   setup:'A mouse is immunised with sheep red blood cells. Four days later its spleen is removed, teased through a mesh into a single-cell suspension, and counted. A measured number of spleen cells is mixed with a large excess of sheep erythrocytes in agar held molten at about 45 °C, and the mixture is poured as a thin layer over a pre-set agar base in a Petri dish, where it gels within seconds and immobilises everything. The dish is incubated for an hour at 37 °C so that antibody secreted by any one cell accumulates in the gel immediately around it rather than diffusing away. Guinea-pig serum is then flooded over the layer as a source of complement. Wherever locally secreted IgM has coated the surrounding sheep cells, complement lyses them, and a clear round hole 0.1–1 mm across appears in the red lawn. The plaques are counted by eye against a dark ground. Dresser and Wortis later added a developing antiserum step to reveal IgG-secreting cells, whose antibody fixes complement too weakly to lyse on its own.',
   meas:'Spleen from an unimmunised mouse gives of order 50 plaque-forming cells per 10⁶ nucleated cells — a real background, not noise. After immunisation the count rises by two to three orders of magnitude, peaking at day 4–5, and falls again over the following week. Plaque number is linear in the number of spleen cells plated over roughly two decades, which is the internal check that each plaque has one origin.',
   infer:'One plaque, one antibody-secreting cell; therefore the number of plaques is the number of such cells; therefore the expansion postulated by clonal selection is an integer that can be counted rather than a titre that must be interpreted. The step that is not deductive is the second one: it requires that every secreting cell in the plated sample makes a visible plaque. Plating efficiency was never measured directly, because there was no independent way to count the cells the assay was invented to count.',
   assume:'Complement must be in excess (titrated, and the count is flat over a range of complement concentrations). The sheep-cell lawn must be uniform, and plaques must not merge, which is controlled by the linearity of count against cells plated. Cells must not divide during the hour of incubation — safe, since the cycle time is six to eight hours. The assumption that could not be controlled is the threshold: a cell secreting below the rate needed to lyse its neighbours is invisible, so the assay counts cells secreting enough complement-fixing IgM, not cells making antibody. Low-rate and low-affinity secretors are systematically missed, and every quantitative statement in cellular immunology between 1963 and the arrival of flow cytometry inherits that bias.'},
  {n:'One cell, one antibody', who:'Gustav Nossal & Joshua Lederberg, Walter and Eliza Hall Institute, Melbourne', y:'1958', fig:'nossal',
   setup:'Rats are immunised with two distinguishable Salmonella flagellar antigens. Lymph nodes are removed, teased into suspension, and individual cells are drawn up one at a time in a micropipette under a microscope and expelled into a microdroplet of culture medium on a coverslip, the whole array of droplets sealed under paraffin oil so that the few nanolitres do not evaporate and nothing diffuses between them. Each droplet is inspected to confirm it contains exactly one cell. After incubation, motile Salmonella of one serotype are introduced into the droplet and watched by dark-field: if the cell has secreted antibody against that flagellar type, the bacteria stop swimming and clump. The other serotype is then tested in the same droplet. The whole readout is a binary judgement about whether small organisms are still moving, made by eye.',
   meas:'Most isolated cells produced nothing detectable, which is itself the expected result at the precursor frequencies now known. Among the minority that did — a few dozen positives out of many hundreds of cells screened one at a time — every single one immobilised one serotype and left the other swimming. The count of doubly reactive cells was zero, and the positives divided between the two serotypes in roughly the proportion the immunisation would predict.',
   infer:'A lymphocyte makes antibody of one specificity, which is exactly what clonal selection requires and what no template theory can accommodate, since on a template account a cell that has met two antigens should fold globulin around both. The inference is from an absence in a small sample, and it was attacked on precisely that ground at the time.',
   assume:'That each droplet held one cell (verified visually, and this is the assumption the design is built to protect). That the immobilisation test detects the antibody one cell can put into a nanolitre — plausible, but the threshold is unknown. That a cell making both specificities would make enough of each to score: a cell secreting 95% of one and 5% of the other would have been recorded as single. Lederberg himself was clear that the sample was small. The independent confirmation came seven years later from allotype work in rabbits (Pernis, Chiappino, Kelus & Gell 1965), which showed that a single cell expresses one of two allelic immunoglobulin forms and not both — allelic exclusion, a structural mechanism rather than a statistical absence.'},
  {n:'Embryonic and myeloma DNA compared by restriction digest', who:'Nobumichi Hozumi & Susumu Tonegawa, Basel Institute for Immunology', y:'1976', fig:'tonegawa',
   setup:'Two sources of DNA: whole twelve-day BALB/c mouse embryos, which contain no mature lymphocytes and therefore no rearranged immunoglobulin loci, and the MOPC 321 plasmacytoma, a clonal tumour of λ-light-chain-secreting plasma cells in which every genome has been rearranged the same way. Both DNAs are digested to completion with the restriction endonuclease BamHI and the fragments separated by size. Each size fraction is then tested by hybridisation against two radiolabelled probes made from purified λ light-chain messenger RNA: the whole mRNA, and a fragment corresponding to its 3′ half, which encodes the constant region. The logic is that the whole-mRNA probe reports where either half of the gene lives, while the half probe reports only the constant half; comparing them locates the variable half. Southern\'s blotting method had been published the year before and was not yet routine, so the fractionation and hybridisation were done as separate steps.',
   meas:'Embryonic DNA, probed with whole λ messenger RNA, gave two distinct hybridising size classes, of roughly six and four kilobases. Probed with the constant-region half only, the same embryonic digest lit up one of those two classes and not the other, which assigns the variable half to the other band. Myeloma DNA gave neither: a single new band of about 2.4 kilobases, hybridising with both the whole probe and the constant-region half.',
   infer:'In the germline the variable and constant sequences sit on different restriction fragments; in the antibody-producing cell they sit on one. The DNA between them has been excised and the ends joined, in a somatic cell, as part of normal development. That conclusion does not follow deductively from a change in fragment size: a band could move because a restriction site has been methylated, or because of a deletion unrelated to joining. What licenses the inference is that the new band hybridises with both probes, which places the two sequences on one fragment rather than merely altering one of them.',
   assume:'That BamHI cuts both genomes at the same sites except where the sequence has changed — site methylation could not be assayed at the time and is the assumption that could not be controlled. That the mRNA preparation is pure λ message. That the plasmacytoma genome is otherwise normal, which is doing real work, since plasmacytomas are aneuploid and carry other rearrangements. Concordant results with several enzymes, and the choice of a clonal tumour so that the result is one cell\'s genome rather than a population average, are the controls that were run. The experiment was correct, and it remains the clean case of a molecular prediction — two genes, one polypeptide — made eleven years before an instrument existed to test it.'},
  {n:'Actively acquired tolerance in neonatal mice', who:'Billingham, Brent & Medawar, University College London', y:'1953', fig:null,
   setup:'A suspension of about 10⁷ living cells from spleen, kidney and testis of a CBA-strain mouse is injected into A-strain mice in utero or within hours of birth. The animals are reared to six to eight weeks. Each then receives a full-thickness skin graft of about 8 mm² from a CBA donor, set orthotopically on the flank and held under a plaster cast for eight days, and a second graft from an unrelated third strain. Grafts are inspected daily thereafter. The readout is not only histology: CBA mice are agouti and A mice albino, so a surviving donor graft announces itself by growing pigmented hair on a white mouse, which is about as direct an observable as transplantation biology provides.',
   meas:'CBA grafts on injected A mice survived indefinitely — beyond 100 days in many animals, and for the life of the mouse in some, growing donor-coloured hair throughout. The same grafts on uninjected A controls were rejected at a median of about 11 days, with the usual sequence of oedema, vascular shutdown and sloughing. Third-party grafts placed on the very same tolerant animals were rejected on the normal schedule, and the yield was strongly dose-dependent: too small an inoculum, or one given too late after birth, produced nothing.',
   infer:'Tolerance is acquired rather than inherited, is specific to the antigens actually encountered, and is induced only during a developmental window that closes within days of birth. The rejection of the third-party graft is what carries the whole argument: without it, indefinite survival of the donor graft is equally consistent with general immunosuppression or with a sick animal, and specificity is the entire theoretical content of the result. The window is what converts a curiosity about neonates into a claim about how the repertoire is built.',
   assume:'That the inoculum persists — chimerism was demonstrated later, not at the time. That the strain disparities are of comparable strength, which is not exactly true, since different H-2 mismatches provoke rejection at different rates. That surviving hair growth means an immunologically intact graft and not a passively surviving remnant. The assumption that could not be controlled is the one that matters most in retrospect: the experiment shows an absence of response, and an absence is consistent with clonal deletion, with anergy, and with active suppression by persisting donor-induced regulatory cells. Burnet read it as deletion because his theory said deletion. All three turn out to be occurring, and the distinction was not experimentally accessible until the 1990s.'},
  {n:'Counting the naive precursors with peptide–MHC tetramers', who:'Moon, Chu, Pepper and Marc Jenkins, Minnesota', y:'2007', fig:null,
   setup:'Recombinant MHC class II molecules are folded around a single chosen peptide, biotinylated, and assembled in fours on a fluorophore-conjugated streptavidin so that four weak interactions become one avid one. Spleen and all lymph nodes of an unimmunised mouse are pooled — of order 10⁸ cells — and stained. The tetramer-binding cells are then pulled out of that suspension on anti-fluorophore magnetic microbeads passed over a column, an enrichment of roughly a thousandfold, and the entire bound fraction is run through a flow cytometer with a counting standard so that an absolute number, not a percentage, comes out. The enrichment step is the whole experiment: at a starting frequency of one in a million the population sits below the false-positive rate of the cytometer and cannot be seen at all.',
   meas:'Across a panel of peptide–MHC class II specificities, the number of naive CD4 T cells in an entire unimmunised mouse ranged from about 20 to about 200 — that is, between one in ten million and one in a million of the animal\'s T cells. The numbers were reproducible between individual mice of the same strain to within about a factor of two, and the immunodominance hierarchy observed weeks later after immunisation tracked these naive precursor numbers rather than the affinities or the abundance of the peptides themselves.',
   infer:'The repertoire is an extremely sparse sample of the space it is drawn from, selection operates on populations of tens of cells, and part of the magnitude of a future response is fixed before the antigen arrives. This is the measurement that makes the ten-day latency of a primary response an arithmetical necessity rather than a defect.',
   assume:'That tetramer binding is a faithful proxy for productive recognition. It is not quite: tetramers preferentially capture higher-affinity clones, and lower-affinity cells that do participate in a real response are undercounted, so the figure is a lower bound. That recovery over the column is near-complete — measured by spiking known numbers of transgenic cells and re-counting. That spleen and lymph nodes hold the whole repertoire, which is the uncontrolled part, since blood, marrow, gut and lung reservoirs are not sampled. The direction of every one of these errors is the same, which is worth saying: the true precursor numbers are somewhat higher than the published ones, and the qualitative conclusion is unaffected.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'Each lymphocyte expresses one receptor specificity.', k:'empirical', n:'Enforced by allelic and isotypic exclusion. Not universal: of order one to three per cent of T cells carry two functional α chains, and dual-receptor cells are implicated in some autoimmunity. The field treats these as noise rather than as counterexamples, which is a choice and not a finding.'},
  {a:'Equilibrium affinity measured in vitro is the quantity selection acts on in vivo.', k:'idealisation', n:'What a germinal-centre B cell is actually selected on is how much antigen it can pull off a follicular dendritic cell and re-present, which depends on off-rate and on mechanical force, not on K_d alone. Affinity and selective value come apart above about 10⁻⁹ M, which is where affinity maturation is observed to stall.'},
  {a:'One plaque, one tetramer event, one sequence read corresponds to one cell.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The counting assumption behind every quantitative claim in cellular immunology. Each version has a known efficiency below one and a known bias in the same direction — toward high secretors, high-affinity binders and abundant clonotypes — so absolute counts are lower bounds and comparisons between methods are not straightforward.'},
  {a:'Antigen and repertoire are well mixed: any clone can meet any antigen.', k:'idealisation', n:'False in an obvious way. Antigen drains to a particular node, and a naive cell recirculates through a given node every twelve to twenty-four hours, so recognition is a search problem in a structured space. This is why adjuvant, route and dose matter as much as they do, and none of it appears in the theory.'},
  {a:'Sequence identity individuates a clonotype.', k:'definition', n:'There is no agreed similarity metric on receptor sequences. Two receptors differing by a framework substitution are functionally the same; two differing by one residue at the centre of CDR3 can differ in affinity by a thousandfold. Published repertoire sizes differ by seven orders of magnitude largely because of this, and none of the authors is making an error.'},
  {a:'Somatic hypermutation is effectively random within the variable region.', k:'idealisation', n:'AID targets WRCY and RGYW motifs, which are enriched several-fold in the complementarity-determining regions. The mutational input is therefore biased toward the positions where a change can help — a mutator shaped by prior natural selection to make a second selective process search efficiently.'},
  {a:'Results in inbred, specific-pathogen-free laboratory mice transfer to outbred humans with immunological histories.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The workhorse assumption of the whole discipline, and it is quantitatively wrong. Co-housing laboratory mice with pet-store mice shifts their memory-cell compartments toward the adult human pattern and changes responses to infection and to vaccination. Most published mouse immunology is the immunology of an animal that has never met anything.'},
  {a:'The extension of "self" is fixed by exposure during a developmental window.', k:'contested', n:'Burnet\'s operational definition, and demonstrably wrong in both directions: sequestered antigens are self and never purged, while the microbiota and the fetus are foreign and untouched. Every successor proposal — infectious non-self, danger, discontinuity — replaces the fixing mechanism rather than the concept, and the dispute is about which physical correlate does the work.'},
  {a:'The primary and secondary responses differ only in the starting clone size and the activation threshold.', k:'idealisation', n:'A useful simplification that ignores tissue residence, pre-switched isotype, epigenetic state and the separate compartment of long-lived plasma cells in the marrow. Only the last of these protects immediately, which is why serological correlates of protection and cellular ones do not agree.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`K_d=\frac{k_{\rm off}}{k_{\rm on}}`, r:'"K-d, the dissociation constant"; "k-off over k-on"',
   m:'The antigen concentration at which half the binding sites are occupied at equilibrium. Small K_d means tight binding, which is the opposite of the intuitive direction and a standing source of confusion. The distinction it marks is thermodynamic: K_d says nothing about how long a complex lasts, and two ligands with identical K_d can have off-rates differing hundredfold — which is what T-cell discrimination actually reads.', d:'K_d in M (mol L⁻¹); k_on in M⁻¹ s⁻¹; k_off in s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\theta=\frac{[\mathrm{Ag}]}{K_d+[\mathrm{Ag}]}`, r:'"theta", fractional occupancy',
   m:'The fraction of receptor sites bound at a given free antigen concentration. It is the bridge between a molecular constant and a cellular event, and the point where an idealisation enters: the cell does not respond to occupancy but to a signal integrated over time with a threshold, so θ is a proxy for the thing that matters, not the thing itself.', d:'dimensionless; [Ag] in M'},
  {s:R`D_{\rm comb},\ D_{\rm junc},\ D_{\rm total}`, r:'"combinatorial, junctional and total diversity"',
   m:'Counts of *possible* receptor sequences, not of receptors present. D_comb is the bookkeeping of which germline segments join which; D_junc is the far larger contribution from nucleotide loss and untemplated addition at the joints. The distinction the notation marks is between a space and a sample: a human carries of order 10⁹–10¹⁰ distinct heavy chains out of 10¹¹–10¹⁸ possible ones.', d:'dimensionless (counts)'},
  {s:R`V_H,\ D,\ J_H,\ V_L,\ J_L`, r:'"V-heavy, D, J-heavy; V-light, J-light"',
   m:'Counts of functional germline gene segments available to the heavy and light chains: roughly 40, 23 and 6 for the human heavy chain. Note the collision the field tolerates: D here is a diversity *segment* between V and J, while D elsewhere on this sheet is a diversity *count*. Light chains have no D segment, which is why they contribute less junctional variation.', d:'dimensionless (counts)'},
  {s:R`\mu_{\rm SHM}`, r:'"mu-S-H-M", the somatic hypermutation rate',
   m:'About 10⁻³ per base per cell division across the rearranged variable region, against a genomic background near 10⁻⁹. Six orders of magnitude of deliberate, locally targeted mutagenesis, switched on by AID in the germinal centre and off again outside it. The symbol marks a rate that is a regulated physiological variable rather than a constant of nature.', d:'per base per division'},
  {s:R`N_{\rm clone}(t)=N_0\,2^{t/T_d}`, r:'"N-clone of t equals N-nought times two to the t over T-d"',
   m:'Clonal expansion as pure doubling. N₀ is the number of precursor cells, of order 20–200 for a given peptide–MHC in a mouse; T_d is the doubling time, six to eight hours at the peak of a primary response. The formula is a deliberate idealisation — it ignores the lag before first division and the contraction phase — and it is what converts a selective event into a physiological one.', d:'N in cells; T_d in hours'},
  {s:R`\frac{dN_i}{dt}=\big[\beta f(a_i)-\delta\big]N_i`, r:'"d-N-i by d-t"; "beta", "delta"',
   m:'Germinal-centre dynamics for clone i: β is the per-cell division rate, δ the death rate, and f the affinity-dependent survival factor. The subscript i is doing the philosophical work — it asserts that the population is partitioned into lineages with distinct identities, which is the clonal-restriction postulate written as an index.', d:'N in cells; β, δ in h⁻¹'},
  {s:R`f(a_i)=a_i/\bar a`, r:'"f of a-i equals a-i over a-bar"',
   m:'Fitness as affinity *relative to the current population mean*, not absolute affinity. This one choice makes germinal-centre selection frequency-dependent and explains why affinity maturation decelerates: as ā rises, the differential on any new variant shrinks. It is soft selection in the population-genetic sense, arrived at independently by immunologists.', d:'a in M⁻¹ (association constant); f dimensionless'},
  {s:R`R_0=\frac{\beta N}{\gamma},\qquad p_c=1-\frac{1}{R_0}`, r:'"R-nought"; "p-c", the critical coverage',
   m:'The exit from immunology into epidemiology. Here β is a transmission rate and N a host population size — note the collision with β as a lymphocyte division rate two entries above, which is standard and unremarked in the literature. p_c is the fraction that must be immune for transmission to fail: 92–95% for measles at R₀ ≈ 12–18.', d:'R₀ and p_c dimensionless; β per contact per time; γ in time⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\text{precursor frequency}\ \sim10^{-5}\text{–}10^{-6}`, r:'"precursor frequency"',
   m:'The fraction of the naive repertoire able to recognise a given epitope. Measured directly by tetramer enrichment since 2007, and inferred before that from limiting dilution, which gave systematically lower numbers. The quantity marks the difference between the repertoire\'s coverage and its depth: almost everything is recognised by almost nothing.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`[\mathrm{Ag}]`, r:'"antigen concentration"',
   m:'Free antigen concentration in whatever compartment is being modelled. The bracket notation imports a well-mixed-solution idealisation from physical chemistry into a system whose antigen is mostly immobilised on follicular dendritic cell surfaces at a local density, not dissolved. Where the idealisation fails is exactly where germinal-centre selection happens.', d:'M (mol L⁻¹)'},
  {s:R`P(\text{in-frame})=1-(\tfrac{2}{3})^2=\tfrac{5}{9}`, r:'"probability at least one allele rearranges in frame"',
   m:'Untemplated nucleotide addition shifts the reading frame in two joints out of three, so each allele has a one-in-three chance of a productive rearrangement and a cell has two tries. The notation records the price of generating specificity at random: roughly 90–95% of developing thymocytes die, most of them for failing arithmetic rather than failing selection.', d:'dimensionless'}
 ],
 worked:{
  t:'Can a hundred cells stop a bacterium? The arithmetic of clonal expansion',
  steps:[
   'Start from the measured precursor number. Tetramer enrichment gives of order 100 naive T cells specific for a given peptide–MHC in an entire mouse — call it N₀ = 100, in an animal with roughly 2×10⁸ T cells, so a precursor frequency of 5×10⁻⁷.',
   'The adversary: a bacterium with a 30-minute doubling time goes from 10³ to 10⁹ organisms in about 10 hours. Selection by itself is therefore hopeless — by the time a hundred cells have bound anything, the infection is over.',
   'Add proliferation. Priming takes about 24–36 hours before the first division. From t = 1.5 d to t = 7 d is 5.5 days = 132 hours; at a measured in vivo doubling time of 7 hours that is 132/7 ≈ 19 divisions.',
   'Expansion factor 2¹⁹ = 5.2×10⁵. Final clone size 100 × 5.2×10⁵ ≈ 5×10⁷ cells.',
   'Compare with measurement: at the peak of a mouse response to an immunodominant LCMV epitope, CD8 T cells of that specificity number of order 10⁷ in the spleen, and a few times that in the whole animal. The calculation is right to within a factor of a few, and the discrepancy is in the expected direction, since not every cell divides on every cycle and killing begins before the peak.',
   'Now the contraction. Between 90 and 95% of the expanded population dies over the following two to three weeks, leaving of order 10⁶ memory cells — still about 10⁴ times the naive number.',
   'Run the same arithmetic from a memory starting point of 10⁶: to reach 5×10⁷ needs 2^5.6, about six divisions, or roughly 1.7 days rather than 5.5 — and memory cells have a lower activation threshold and need less help, so the lag before the first division is shorter too.'],
  a:'About 5×10⁷ effector cells from 100 precursors in a week, and about 2–3 days rather than 10 on a second encounter. Three things fall out of one calculation. The ten-day latency of a primary response is not a design fault; it is what 19 doublings take. Vaccination works by changing N₀ and nothing else about the mechanism, which is why it was possible to do it for two centuries without understanding it. And the system is committed to building, every time, a population of tens of millions of cells that did not exist a week earlier and will mostly be dead a month later — the contraction phase is as constitutive of the theory as the expansion, and it is the part no popular account mentions.'}
},

physio: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1628', who:'William Harvey, London', t:'A mass balance kills a doctrine',
   b:'*De Motu Cordis* does not argue from anatomy alone; it argues from arithmetic. Harvey estimates the volume the left ventricle ejects per beat — he is deliberately conservative, taking as little as half an ounce — multiplies by a beat rate, and finds that the heart puts out more than the entire blood volume in a few minutes, and far more in an hour than the liver could conceivably manufacture from food. Therefore the same blood must return. He had no capillaries: Malpighi saw them in 1661, four years after Harvey died, so the connection between arteries and veins was a required posit, not an observation. The founding move of quantitative physiology is a conservation argument used to rule out a theory whose direct evidence had not been overturned.'},
  {y:'1840s–1855', who:'Claude Bernard, Paris', t:'The liver makes sugar',
   b:'Bernard set out to find where ingested sugar is destroyed and found instead that it is made. A dog fed only meat still has sugar in its hepatic vein. He then washed an excised liver free of sugar with water until none could be detected, left it overnight, and found sugar again — so the tissue itself produces it, from a stored precursor he isolated and named glycogen. The animal is not merely a consumer of what plants build. This is the first demonstration of an internal secretion and, more importantly for the theory, the first demonstration of a regulated internal supply: something inside the organism is holding a variable up against consumption.'},
  {y:'1865–1878', who:'Bernard', t:'Determinism, and the fixity of the internal environment',
   b:'The *Introduction à l\'étude de la médecine expérimentale* is a methodological treatise arguing that biology admits no exception to physical determinism and that the experimenter\'s job is to hold everything fixed but one thing — written against Bichat\'s vital force and against the clinical habit of arguing from series of cases. The formulation that became the field\'s motto came later, in the *Leçons sur les phénomènes de la vie* of 1878: the constancy of the internal environment is the condition of free and independent life. The emphasis belongs on *libre*. Bernard\'s claim is not that internal variables happen to be steady but that holding them steady is what buys an animal independence from its surroundings.'},
  {y:'1883–1895', who:'Max Rubner, Munich; Otto Frank', t:'The first scaling law, and the first length–tension curve',
   b:'Rubner measured heat production in dogs of different sizes and found it proportional to body surface area, hence to M^(2/3) — a quantitative biological law with a mechanism, since a warm body loses heat across its surface. It held for half a century. In the same decade Frank, working on isolated frog hearts, showed that the force of contraction depends on the degree of filling before it, plotting the first ventricular function curves. Neither man was doing homeostasis; both supplied the quantitative style that made it possible to state homeostasis as something other than a slogan.'},
  {y:'1912–1918', who:'Ernest Starling and F. P. Knowlton, University College London', t:'Regulation without a regulator',
   b:'The heart–lung preparation isolates a dog\'s heart and lungs from the rest of the animal and from all nervous control, and lets the experimenter set filling pressure and arterial resistance independently. Under those conditions the heart still matches its output to what is delivered to it. Starling gave this as the Linacre Lecture in 1915: "the law of the heart", that the energy of contraction is a function of the length of the muscle fibre. The philosophical importance is that a regulatory behaviour is exhibited in a preparation that contains no controller — the appearance of purpose comes out of a mechanical property of the tissue, which is exactly the demonstration a mechanistic physiology needs.'},
  {y:'1926–1932', who:'Walter Cannon, Harvard', t:'Homeostasis named',
   b:'Cannon coined the word in 1926 and chose the prefix carefully: *homeo-*, similar, not *homo-*, same, because the regulated variables oscillate. *The Wisdom of the Body* (1932) sets out the postulates — the organism resists change, the resistance is active, the mechanisms are multiple and often antagonistic, and constancy is an achievement — and does so in a vocabulary of bodily wisdom that Cannon meant as a figure of speech and that the field then read literally for fifty years. Cannon\'s own experimental contribution, the sympatho-adrenal emergency response, is an account of how the internal environment is deliberately *destabilised* under threat, which sits awkwardly with the slogan and anticipates allostasis by sixty years.'},
  {y:'1942–1959', who:'Kuhn & Ryffel, Basel; Wirz, Hargitay & Kuhn; Gottschalk & Mylle, North Carolina', t:'A mechanism predicted before it could be measured',
   b:'Werner Kuhn was a physical chemist who had worked on isotope separation by countercurrent methods. Looking at the hairpin geometry of the loop of Henle, he and Ryffel proposed in 1942 that a small transverse gradient applied repeatedly along counterflowing limbs would multiply into a large axial one. They had no renal data at all. Wirz measured the gradient in frozen kidney slices in 1951 and found it; Gottschalk and Mylle punctured individual tubules in hamsters in 1959 and found the specific signature that distinguishes multiplication from simple osmotic equilibration, namely hypotonic fluid entering the distal tubule while the final urine is concentrated. It is one of very few cases in biology of a correct mechanism derived from engineering before the measurement existed.'},
  {y:'1972–2010', who:'Arthur Guyton, Mississippi; Sterling & Eyer; Levick & Michel', t:'A model, an amendment and a retraction',
   b:'Guyton\'s 1972 circulatory model — 354 blocks on an analogue computer — made long-term arterial pressure a property of the kidney\'s pressure–natriuresis relation, with effectively infinite feedback gain, and therefore made every other mechanism a transient. It remains the framework for the field\'s central dispute and it has never been decisively tested. Sterling and Eyer proposed allostasis in 1988: set points are themselves adjusted in anticipation, so constancy is a means and not an end. And in 2010 Levick and Michel showed that the century-old textbook account of capillary fluid exchange — filtration at one end, reabsorption at the other — is wrong in most tissues, because the oncotic pressure that matters is that of the sub-glycocalyx space. A discipline old enough to be settled, correcting a diagram every medical student had drawn.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'Localisation by exclusion: curare and the pancreatic fistula', who:'Claude Bernard, Collège de France', y:'1848–1856', fig:'curare',
   setup:'Two preparations built on one idea — isolate a function surgically until only one explanation survives. For curare: a ligature is tied around the vessels of one hind limb of a frog so that the limb receives no blood, and the animal is then poisoned systemically with curare, an arrow poison Bernard obtained from travellers returning from South America. Once the animal is paralysed, the ligated limb is tested for reflex movement, the sciatic nerve of a paralysed limb is stimulated electrically, and then the muscle of the same limb is stimulated directly at matched current. A further preparation checks that a curarised nerve still conducts, by stimulating it above the poisoned region and recording from an unpoisoned muscle. In a mammal the animal must be ventilated by bellows throughout, because curare kills by paralysing the diaphragm while leaving the animal conscious — a fact Bernard established and described. For the pancreas: a permanent fistula is made to bring pancreatic duct juice to the surface of a living dog, so that the secretion can be collected in quantity and tested on substrates outside the animal.',
   meas:'The ligated limb retains reflex movement and contracts when its nerve is stimulated, while every unligated limb is inert to nerve stimulation yet contracts fully to direct stimulation of the muscle at the same current. Nerve conduction through a curarised segment is unimpaired. Collected pancreatic juice emulsifies neutral fat and splits it into fatty acid and glycerol, which neither saliva nor gastric juice does; it also digests starch and protein.',
   infer:'Curare abolishes neither nerve nor muscle but the passage between them, so there exists a distinct site with its own pharmacology — a structure defined by a drug before anatomy could show it. Fat digestion is a pancreatic function, not a gastric one, which relocates a physiological capacity to an organ by collecting its product rather than by removing it. Neither inference is deductive: localisation by exclusion is only as good as the list of candidate sites, and in 1856 the neuromuscular junction was not on anyone\'s list.',
   assume:'That the ligature excludes the poison without otherwise disabling the limb — controlled by showing that ligated limbs in unpoisoned frogs behave normally, and that releasing the ligature paralyses the limb on schedule. That stimulus strengths are matched between nerve and muscle. That the fistula\'s juice is representative of what reaches the duodenum, which is the weak point: a chronic fistula diverts the secretion from its normal stimulus and its normal mixture with bile, and Bernard knew the preparation altered the animal. The assumption that could not be controlled is the one his method depends on throughout — that a drug or a lesion has a single site of action.'},
  {n:'The heart–lung preparation', who:'Ernest Starling with Knowlton, Patterson and Piper, University College London', y:'1912–1914', fig:'heartlung',
   setup:'An anaesthetised dog is opened, the blood anticoagulated — heparin did not yet exist — and the systemic circulation replaced by an external circuit while the heart continues to perfuse its own coronaries and to pump through the lungs, which are ventilated artificially. Venous return arrives from a heated reservoir whose height above the right atrium sets the filling pressure, adjustable by raising or lowering it. Aortic outflow passes through a screw-clamped rubber tube — a variable resistance — back to the reservoir, so the afterload can be set independently of the preload. The whole circuit is water-jacketed at 38–40 °C. Ventricular volume is recorded by a cardiometer: a rigid chamber sealed around the ventricles whose enclosed air is connected to a piston writing on a smoked drum, so that each beat inscribes a volume trace. Output is measured by timed collection.',
   meas:'Raising the reservoir raises filling pressure, end-diastolic volume and stroke volume together; cardiac output tracks venous return over roughly a three- to fourfold range with arterial pressure held constant. Raising the outflow resistance at constant venous return enlarges the diastolic volume and the heart then ejects against the higher pressure with little change in output. At the highest filling pressures output falls again.',
   infer:'The heart is a demand-following pump whose output is set by its filling, intrinsically, with no nervous input. The step that is not deductive is the extrapolation to the intact animal. In an exercising dog cardiac output can triple while end-diastolic volume *falls*, because sympathetic drive shifts the entire function curve; so the Frank–Starling relation is real, is demonstrated exactly as claimed, and is not the mechanism operating during exercise. A century of teaching has run those two things together.',
   assume:'That the preparation is stable across the manipulations — controlled by returning to the starting filling pressure and checking that output repeats, which Starling did and reported deteriorating over hours. That the cardiometer does not itself restrict filling, which it does somewhat. That coronary perfusion is adequate at every aortic pressure used, which it is not at the lowest ones, and this is a standing reason to distrust the descending limb of the curve. The assumption that could not be controlled: that the intrinsic behaviour of an isolated, denervated, hirudinised heart at fixed temperature is the intrinsic behaviour of the heart in an animal.'},
  {n:'Micropuncture of the nephron', who:'Wirz, Hargitay & Kuhn, Basel (1951); Gottschalk & Mylle, Chapel Hill (1959)', y:'1951–1959', fig:'micropunc',
   setup:'Wirz\'s method: a kidney is frozen rapidly in liquid air, sectioned at successive depths from cortex to papilla, and the melting point of each slice measured cryoscopically, giving osmolality from freezing-point depression as a function of depth. Gottschalk and Mylle then went inside the tubules. Golden hamsters were used because the renal papilla protrudes into the pelvis and can be exposed. The anaesthetised animal\'s kidney is immobilised in a cup, the ureter opened to expose the papilla, and the surface transilluminated. Glass micropipettes are drawn to tips of six to ten micrometres, sharpened, and filled with stained mineral oil; a micromanipulator advances one into an identified tubule under a stereomicroscope. A few nanolitres of fluid are withdrawn by suction, with an oil block placed upstream to stop flow during collection. The sample\'s osmolality is measured by freezing-point depression on a cooled microscope stage. The site of each puncture is established afterwards by injecting the nephron with latex or Microfil and microdissecting it out.',
   meas:'Tissue and tubular osmolality rise together from about 290–300 mOsm/kg in the cortex to several times that at the papillary tip — hamsters concentrate far better than humans, reaching values of order 2000–3000 mOsm/kg. At every depth, fluid inside the descending limb and the surrounding interstitium matched to within the measurement error. The decisive observation is elsewhere: fluid sampled in the early distal tubule was *hypotonic* to plasma, of order 100–150 mOsm/kg, in animals whose final urine was strongly concentrated.',
   infer:'The descending limb equilibrates passively with an interstitium that something else has concentrated; the ascending limb removes solute without water and therefore delivers dilute fluid downstream; the final concentration is achieved by abstracting water in the collecting duct as it passes back through the gradient. The hypotonic early distal sample is what kills the alternative: a kidney that simply concentrated by osmotic equilibration would deliver isotonic fluid there. The non-deductive step is directional — equality of tubular and interstitial osmolality at a point does not by itself say which is concentrating which, and the direction comes from the ascending limb\'s output plus the independent knowledge that it spends ATP.',
   assume:'That exposing, immobilising and puncturing the kidney leaves it functioning — checked by comparing whole-kidney urine from punctured and unpunctured sides. That the oil block does not change flow upstream during the collection, which it does, so collections are kept short. That nanolitre cryoscopy is accurate, calibrated against standards. That a surgically opened anaesthetised animal is in a normal antidiuretic state, which it is not quite, since surgery itself releases vasopressin. The assumption that could not be controlled is the sampling one: only superficial nephrons can be punctured, and these have short loops, while the deep juxtamedullary nephrons that actually build most of the gradient are unreachable.'},
  {n:'Cardiac output by the Fick principle', who:'Cournand & Richards, Bellevue Hospital, New York (after Forssmann, 1929)', y:'1941–1945', fig:null,
   setup:'A radio-opaque ureteric catheter is introduced into an antecubital vein and advanced under fluoroscopy through the right atrium and right ventricle into the pulmonary artery — the manoeuvre Werner Forssmann had performed on himself in Eberswalde in 1929 and been dismissed from his post for. A blood sample is drawn from the catheter tip as the mixed venous sample, and simultaneously an arterial sample by radial or brachial puncture. Over the same interval the subject breathes through a valve into a Douglas bag for three to five minutes at rest, and the collected gas is analysed for oxygen and carbon dioxide by Haldane apparatus. The oxygen content of both blood samples is measured by the Van Slyke manometric method, which extracts the gas chemically and reads its volume, with a precision of a few per cent.',
   meas:'A resting adult: oxygen uptake about 250 mL/min at standard temperature and pressure, dry; arterial oxygen content 190–200 mL per litre of blood; mixed venous 140–150 mL/L; arteriovenous difference therefore about 45–50 mL/L. Dividing gives a cardiac output of about 5 L/min, or a cardiac index near 3 L min⁻¹ m⁻² of body surface. Repeat determinations in the same resting subject agree to within a few per cent; agreement with indicator-dilution and, later, thermodilution methods is looser, within 10–20%, and the discrepancy is systematic rather than random.',
   infer:'A flow is obtained from three concentrations and a rate, with no model of the heart whatever — this is mass balance solved for an unknown, and its independence from any cardiac theory is exactly its value. The step that is not deductive is the identification of pulmonary-artery blood with "mixed venous" blood: it is mixed only to the extent that the right ventricle mixes, and streaming from the coronary sinus and from the two venae cavae is incomplete.',
   assume:'That the subject is in a steady state across the collection — controlled by repeating the measurement and by checking that the respiratory exchange ratio is stable, and violated by any anxiety-driven hyperventilation, which is not a small effect in a person with a catheter in their heart. That there is no intracardiac or intrapulmonary shunt; in a patient with a left-to-right shunt the pulmonary Fick output exceeds the systemic one, which is how shunts came to be quantified. That gas collection is complete and the Van Slyke calibration holds. The assumption that could not be controlled is the one that troubles every invasive measurement: that catheterising the heart does not change the output being measured.'},
  {n:'Kleiber\'s metabolic dataset', who:'Max Kleiber, Davis, California', y:'1932', fig:null,
   setup:'Not an apparatus so much as a compilation with a standard. Kleiber assembled basal or standard metabolic rates for thirteen groups of mammals and birds spanning from a ring dove of about 0.15 kg to a steer of about 680 kg — nearly four orders of magnitude of mass. The underlying measurements are indirect calorimetry: oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production recorded in a closed or open-circuit chamber, converted to heat production using the appropriate caloric equivalent of oxygen for the substrate mixture. The methodological work is entirely in the qualifiers. "Basal" requires the animal post-absorptive, at rest, awake, and within its thermoneutral zone — and the thermoneutral zone has to be determined separately for each species before the measurement can be made at all. For a ruminant, "post-absorptive" has no meaning, since fermentation never stops.',
   meas:'A least-squares fit of log heat production against log body mass gives a slope of 0.74, with individual points scattering by of order ten to twenty per cent about the line. Samuel Brody\'s independent compilation of the same period gave 0.73. Kleiber rounded to three-quarters for arithmetical convenience and said in print that he was doing so.',
   infer:'Metabolic rate is strongly sublinear in body mass, with an exponent closer to 3/4 than to Rubner\'s 2/3. The step that is not deductive, and which the field has spent ninety years on, is the move from a fitted slope over four decades of mass to an exponent, and from an exponent to a mechanism. Over any single dataset of this range the confidence interval on the slope comfortably contains both 0.67 and 0.75; discriminating them requires either a wider mass range, which imports taxa with different body temperatures and different meanings of "basal", or tighter per-species control, which shrinks the range.',
   assume:'That basal metabolic rate is one comparable quantity across a dove and a steer. It is not: body temperature varies by several degrees across the sample, and with a Q₁₀ of two to three that alone shifts rate by roughly ten per cent per degree. That species are independent data points — they are not, and phylogenetic non-independence was not correctable until Felsenstein\'s method in 1985. That the relation is a power law at all: Kolokotrones and colleagues fitted a quadratic in log mass in 2010 and found significant curvature, with the local slope rising from about 0.67 in small mammals to above 0.75 in large ones. The assumption that cannot be controlled is definitional: there is no species-independent operational definition of "basal", so the exponent depends on curation, which is why careful re-analyses keep landing on different numbers without anyone making an error.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'Each compartment is well mixed and instantaneously uniform.', k:'idealisation', n:'Required by every clearance, dilution and Fick computation. Mixing times in plasma are of order a minute and in the interstitium far longer, so any measurement made on a shorter timescale than the mixing it presupposes is reporting a transient. This is the main reason single-injection and continuous-infusion clearance methods disagree.'},
  {a:'The organism is in steady state for the duration of the measurement.', k:'idealisation', n:'Nothing in physiology is in steady state; the question is only whether the drift over the collection is small relative to the quantity. Fick outputs, GFR determinations and indirect calorimetry all fail silently under this assumption rather than giving an obviously wrong answer, which is the dangerous failure mode.'},
  {a:'Blood is a Newtonian fluid in steady laminar flow through rigid cylindrical tubes.', k:'idealisation', n:'False everywhere it matters except the arterioles. The aorta has a Womersley number of 15–20 and peak Reynolds numbers in the thousands; below about 300 μm the apparent viscosity falls as red cells file down the axis (Fåhræus–Lindqvist); and no vessel is rigid. It survives because the r⁻⁴ dependence is qualitatively robust and because resistance is in fact set where the assumption nearly holds.'},
  {a:'An anaesthetised, surgically instrumented animal is physiologically normal.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Anaesthesia roughly halves baroreflex gain, suppresses thermoregulation, alters renal blood flow and releases vasopressin. Most of classical cardiovascular and renal physiology was measured under it. The direction of the error is usually known and rarely corrected for.'},
  {a:'Every regulated variable has a physically realised comparator holding a reference value.', k:'contested', n:'Demonstrable for temperature, where local warming of the preoptic area shifts shivering and sweating thresholds in a graded way. Not demonstrated for body weight: leptin is a signal proportional to fat mass, but no neuron has been shown to compare it against a stored reference. The word "set point" in the obesity literature outruns its evidence.'},
  {a:'Inulin is freely filtered and neither secreted nor reabsorbed, so its clearance is the glomerular filtration rate.', k:'empirical', n:'A genuine empirical claim, not a definition, and checked against ⁵¹Cr-EDTA, iothalamate and iohexol clearances, which agree to within a few per cent. It is the cleanest correspondence rule in physiology, and it is what makes an unobservable flow a measured number.'},
  {a:'Population reference intervals are the individual\'s regulated range.', k:'definition', n:'They are not. An individual\'s own day-to-day variation in plasma sodium, calcium or thyroid hormone is typically several times narrower than the population interval, so a value inside the reference range can be well outside that person\'s regulated range. The conflation is built into every laboratory report.'},
  {a:'Physiological rate scales with body mass as a single power law.', k:'contested', n:'The relation is sublinear across twenty orders of magnitude and that much is not in doubt. Whether one exponent fits is: White & Seymour obtained 0.686 with temperature and gut-content corrections, and Kolokotrones et al. found significant curvature, in which case no exponent is the exponent and any derivation of one derives something that does not exist.'},
  {a:'Doses and physiological parameters may be extrapolated between species by allometric scaling.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Used routinely in pharmacology and in first-in-human dose selection. It works to within a factor of two or three for drugs cleared by bulk processes and fails badly for anything cleared by a specific enzyme whose expression is not conserved, which is most of them.'},
  {a:'A loop can be opened experimentally without changing the system.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The whole gain formalism depends on it: denervating the carotid sinus to measure baroreflex gain assumes that the denervated animal is the intact animal minus one path. It is not — chronic denervation produces compensatory changes within days, so open-loop gains are measured acutely and then applied to chronic questions they may not bear on.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`\frac{d}{dt}(VC)=\dot m_{\rm in}-\dot m_{\rm out}+\dot m_{\rm prod}-\dot m_{\rm cons}`, r:'"d by d-t of V-C"; "m-dot in, out, prod, cons"',
   m:'Mass balance for a compartment of volume V at concentration C. The overdot is a rate, always. This is not a physiological hypothesis but conservation of matter with boundaries drawn on it, and it is the only relation in physiology from which numbers can be extracted with no further assumption — which is why every classical measurement is this identity solved for one unknown.', d:'V in L; C in mol L⁻¹; ṁ in mol s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\dot Q=\frac{\dot V_{\mathrm O_2}}{C_a\mathrm{O_2}-C_{\bar v}\mathrm{O_2}}`, r:'"Q-dot equals V-dot-O-two over C-a-O-two minus C-v-bar-O-two"',
   m:'The Fick principle. Q̇ is cardiac output; the bar over v marks *mixed* venous blood, and that bar is the whole practical difficulty, since obtaining it requires a catheter in the pulmonary artery. Note that C here is a blood gas *content*, not a concentration in the mass-balance sense and not a compliance.', d:'Q̇ in L min⁻¹; V̇O₂ in mL min⁻¹; content in mL O₂ per L blood'},
  {s:R`\Delta x_{\rm closed}=\frac{\Delta x_{\rm open}}{1+G}`, r:'"delta-x closed equals delta-x open over one plus G"',
   m:'G is open-loop gain: the ratio of the error a disturbance produces with the feedback path cut to the error it produces with the loop intact. It is what converts "the body regulates x" into a number, and the numbers are modest — the arterial baroreflex sits at 2–4 acutely and near zero against a sustained disturbance, because it resets.', d:'G dimensionless; Δx in the units of the regulated variable'},
  {s:R`J_v=K_f\big[(P_c-P_i)-\sigma(\pi_c-\pi_g)\big]`, r:'"J-v equals K-f times…"',
   m:'Net fluid flux across a capillary wall. K_f is the filtration coefficient (permeability × area) and σ the reflection coefficient for protein, running from 0 for a freely permeable wall to 1 for a perfect one. The subscript g is the revision: the opposing oncotic pressure is that of the sub-glycocalyx space, not the interstitium, and substituting it removes venular reabsorption from most tissues.', d:'J_v in mL min⁻¹; K_f in mL min⁻¹ mmHg⁻¹; σ dimensionless'},
  {s:R`P_c,\ P_i,\ \pi_c,\ \pi_g`, r:'"capillary and interstitial hydrostatic pressure; capillary and sub-glycocalyx oncotic pressure"',
   m:'Roman P for hydrostatic, Greek π for colloid osmotic — a notational distinction carrying a physical one, since only the oncotic term is weighted by σ. Typical values: P_c 30 mmHg arteriolar falling to 15 venular, P_i slightly negative in many tissues, π_c about 25 mmHg from albumin.', d:'mmHg'},
  {s:R`Q=\frac{\pi\,\Delta P\,r^{4}}{8\eta L},\qquad R=\frac{\Delta P}{Q}\propto r^{-4}`, r:'"Poiseuille"; "R equals delta-P over Q"',
   m:'Volume flow through a rigid tube. η is dynamic viscosity, L length, r radius. Halving the radius multiplies resistance sixteenfold, which is why arterioles and not the aorta set systemic resistance. Note the overloading: r is a radius here and a relatedness coefficient on the ethology sheet; R is resistance here and a gas constant elsewhere.', d:'Q in mL s⁻¹; ΔP in dyn cm⁻² or mmHg; η in Pa s; r, L in cm'},
  {s:R`\mathrm{Re}=\frac{\rho v d}{\eta},\qquad \mathrm{Wo}=r\sqrt{\frac{\omega\rho}{\eta}}`, r:'"Reynolds number"; "Womersley number"',
   m:'The two dimensionless groups that say whether Poiseuille applies. Re compares inertial to viscous forces; Wo compares the oscillation frequency to the time for viscous effects to diffuse across the vessel. Poiseuille needs both small. In the human aorta Re peaks in the thousands and Wo is 15–20, so the aortic pressure drop is inertial and reflective, not viscous — the equation above is simply the wrong one there.', d:'both dimensionless'},
  {s:R`Y=Y_0M^{b}`, r:'"Y equals Y-nought M to the b"',
   m:'The allometric form. M is body mass, b the scaling exponent, Y₀ a normalisation whose units depend on b and which is therefore not comparable between fits with different exponents — a point routinely ignored when "metabolic level" is compared across taxa. The whole dispute is over whether b exists as a constant.', d:'Y in the units of the rate; M in kg; b dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\text{Osm};\ \mathrm{mOsm/kg\ H_2O}`, r:'"osmolality", milliosmoles per kilogram of water',
   m:'Osmolality is per kilogram of solvent, osmolarity per litre of solution; in plasma they differ by about 7% because of the volume occupied by protein and lipid. Physiology uses osmolality because it is what a freezing-point depression measures and what a membrane responds to. Human plasma 285–295; human papillary tip 1200–1400; desert rodents several thousand.', d:'mOsm per kg H₂O'},
  {s:R`\dot V_E=S\,(P_a\mathrm{CO_2}-B)`, r:'"V-dot-E equals S times P-a-C-O-two minus B"',
   m:'The hypercapnic ventilatory response. V̇_E is minute ventilation, S the slope of the response and B the apnoeic threshold — the arterial CO₂ tension below which breathing stops. S varies between healthy individuals from about 1 to 4 L min⁻¹ mmHg⁻¹, a fourfold spread that matters directly for opioid safety and altitude tolerance and that textbook single figures conceal.', d:'V̇_E in L min⁻¹; S in L min⁻¹ mmHg⁻¹; P_aCO₂ and B in mmHg'},
  {s:R`\mathrm{pH}=6.1+\log_{10}\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{0.03\,P_{\mathrm{CO_2}}}`, r:'"Henderson–Hasselbalch"',
   m:'6.1 is the apparent pK of the carbonic acid system in plasma at 37 °C and 0.03 the solubility coefficient converting a CO₂ partial pressure in mmHg to a concentration in mmol/L. Both are empirical constants for one fluid at one temperature, not universal ones, and both drift in the extremes of illness where the equation is most relied upon.', d:'pH dimensionless; [HCO₃⁻] in mmol L⁻¹; P_CO₂ in mmHg'},
  {s:R`C_x=\frac{U_x\dot V}{P_x}`, r:'"clearance of x equals U-x V-dot over P-x"',
   m:'The volume of plasma completely cleared of substance x per unit time — a virtual volume, since no real volume of plasma is ever wholly cleared. U and P are urinary and plasma concentrations and V̇ the urine flow rate. Inulin clearance is GFR at about 125 mL/min; PAH clearance approximates renal plasma flow at about 625 mL/min because a single pass extracts roughly 90% of it.', d:'mL min⁻¹'}
 ],
 worked:{
  t:'What the kidney spends on sodium, and why the stoichiometry does not close',
  steps:[
   'Filtration: GFR is 125 mL/min, so 180 L of plasma is filtered per day. At a plasma sodium of 140 mmol/L, and taking sodium as freely filtered, that is 180 × 140 = 25 200 mmol of Na⁺ presented to the tubules each day — about 580 g, roughly forty times the total body sodium.',
   'Excretion: a person in balance on a Western diet excretes about 150 mmol/day. So 25 050 mmol is reabsorbed, which is 99.4% of what was filtered.',
   'Direct cost of pumping: the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase exports three sodium ions per ATP hydrolysed. Reabsorbing 25 050 mmol by active transport alone would need 25 050/3 ≈ 8 350 mmol ATP per day.',
   'Convert to oxygen: oxidative phosphorylation yields roughly 5–6 ATP per O₂ consumed for a mixed substrate. That is about 1 400–1 700 mmol O₂/day, or 31–38 L/day at standard conditions — 22–26 mL/min.',
   'Now the measurement. Renal blood flow is about 1.1 L/min and the arteriovenous oxygen difference across the kidney is small, around 14 mL per litre of blood, giving a renal oxygen consumption of about 15–18 mL/min — some 7% of the whole-body 250 mL/min, in an organ receiving a fifth to a quarter of the cardiac output.',
   'The two numbers disagree by a factor of about 1.4, and the disagreement is in the wrong direction: the kidney reabsorbs more sodium per oxygen molecule than the pump stoichiometry permits. Expressed as the ratio physiologists actually report, measured Na⁺ reabsorbed per O₂ consumed is about 25–30, while three Na⁺ per ATP and five to six ATP per O₂ caps the active mechanism at 15–18.',
   'The surplus is not error. Roughly two-thirds of proximal sodium reabsorption is paracellular — sodium follows, through the tight junctions and by solvent drag, the electrochemical gradient that the pump has established for the other third. The pump pays for a minority of the sodium it moves.'],
  a:'About 15–18 mL of oxygen a minute to recover 25 mol of sodium a day, and a stoichiometric estimate that overshoots by 40%. The discrepancy is the interesting part. A compartmental mass-balance model gets the flux exactly right and the cost badly wrong, because the flux is an accounting identity while the cost depends on a mechanism — the geometry of a leaky epithelium — that no amount of compartment-drawing contains. It is a small, clean instance of the general point that conservation laws constrain physiology without explaining it, and that the place where the books fail to balance is usually where the biology is.'}
},

etho: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1872–1873', who:'Charles Darwin; Douglas Spalding', t:'Behaviour as an inherited character',
   b:'*The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals* treats behaviour patterns as traits with a phylogeny, comparable across species like bones, and argues from their continuity to common descent. In the following year Spalding, a self-taught tutor working in Scotland, hooded newly hatched chicks so that they could not see, removed the hoods at different ages, and found that a chick would follow whatever moving object it first saw — but only if unhooded within a few days. He had discovered imprinting and the sensitive period, published it in *Macmillan\'s Magazine*, and was then entirely forgotten for sixty years.'},
  {y:'1898–1918', who:'Charles Whitman, Chicago; Oskar Heinroth, Berlin; Jakob von Uexküll', t:'Behaviour as a taxonomic character, and the animal\'s own world',
   b:'Whitman with pigeons and Heinroth with ducks independently concluded that species-specific behaviour patterns are as reliable for classification as morphology — Heinroth\'s comparative study of Anatidae courtship is the direct ancestor of Lorenz\'s method. Von Uexküll\'s *Umwelt und Innenwelt der Tiere* made the complementary point from the other direction: an animal does not live in the world but in the world its receptors and effectors constitute. His tick, which responds to butyric acid, to warmth, and to a hairless patch, and to nothing else, is the founding image of the discipline\'s anti-anthropocentrism.'},
  {y:'1935–1938', who:'Konrad Lorenz, Altenberg; Niko Tinbergen, Leiden', t:'The Kumpan paper, and a partnership',
   b:'Lorenz\'s "Der Kumpan in der Umwelt des Vogels" set out imprinting, the innate releasing mechanism, and the idea that an animal has different "companions" for different functional contexts. Lorenz was an observer of hand-reared birds in a house full of them; Tinbergen was an experimentalist who had already spent years in the dunes moving landmarks around digger-wasp burrows to show that the wasps navigate by visual configuration. They met in Leiden in 1936 and worked together at Altenberg in 1937, where the egg-rolling experiments were done. The complementarity is the reason ethology became a science rather than a natural history: Lorenz supplied the categories, Tinbergen the means of testing them.'},
  {y:'1950–1951', who:'Lorenz; Tinbergen', t:'The instinct models — elaborate, influential, wrong',
   b:'Lorenz\'s psychohydraulic model has action-specific energy accumulating in a reservoir behind a valve that a releaser opens; if it is not discharged the behaviour eventually appears without any stimulus at all, as a vacuum activity. Tinbergen\'s *The Study of Instinct* offers a hierarchy of nested centres, each releasing the next. Both were taken very seriously, both explained the phenomena then in hand — falling thresholds under deprivation, vacuum activities, displacement behaviour — and neither survived. There is no such quantity as action-specific energy, and the hierarchy predicted nothing a simpler threshold account did not. The residue of the first is the folk conviction that aggression builds up and must be vented.'},
  {y:'1953', who:'Daniel Lehrman, Rutgers', t:'The critique from comparative psychology',
   b:'Lehrman\'s "A Critique of Konrad Lorenz\'s Theory of Instinctive Behavior" argued that the innate/learned dichotomy is incoherent because every trait develops, that Lorenz\'s isolation experiment cannot establish innateness (raising an animal without a particular experience does not raise it without experience), and that the energy model was a physiological fiction with no referent. Lehrman also referred to Lorenz\'s writings of the Nazi period, in which Lorenz had used his ethological concepts in support of racial-hygiene policy — a matter the field has addressed unevenly. The scientific objection was never satisfactorily answered; the response was to stop asking whether traits are innate and to start asking how they develop, which is Tinbergen\'s second question.'},
  {y:'1963', who:'Niko Tinbergen', t:'Four questions, three pages',
   b:'"On Aims and Methods of Ethology", written for Lorenz\'s sixtieth birthday, distinguishes causation, ontogeny, survival value and evolution, and insists that answers to different questions are not rivals. The paper asserts almost nothing about animals. Its content is entirely about what kind of answer a given question demands — a piece of philosophy of science produced by a working scientist, and the most durable thing the field has made. Every substantive theory in Tinbergen\'s own book had been replaced within twenty years; the taxonomy of questions has not been.'},
  {y:'1964–1976', who:'Hamilton; Williams; Trivers; Maynard Smith & Price; Charnov', t:'The functional question becomes mathematics',
   b:'Hamilton\'s two 1964 papers give inclusive fitness and rb > c. Williams\' *Adaptation and Natural Selection* (1966) demolishes casual group-selectionist reasoning and insists that adaptation is an onerous concept requiring evidence. Trivers supplies reciprocal altruism (1971), parental investment (1972) and parent–offspring conflict (1974). Maynard Smith and Price (1973) import game theory and, in doing so, invent a solution concept requiring no rational agent. Charnov (1976) derives the marginal value theorem. Within twelve years the survival-value question acquired a body of theory that made quantitative, falsifiable predictions, and behavioural ecology separated from ethology.'},
  {y:'1975–1979', who:'E. O. Wilson; Dawkins; Krebs & Davies; Gould & Lewontin', t:'Sociobiology, and the reckoning',
   b:'Wilson\'s *Sociobiology* (1975) synthesised the new theory and added a final chapter on humans that produced years of political conflict, including an organised campaign against him by colleagues at Harvard. Dawkins\' *The Selfish Gene* (1976) gave the gene\'s-eye view its canonical popular statement. Krebs and Davies\' *Behavioural Ecology* (1978) supplied the textbook that named the discipline. And Gould and Lewontin\'s "Spandrels" (1979) attacked the whole adaptationist method for atomising organisms, assuming optimality and inventing untested selective stories. The field\'s answer was methodological rather than rhetorical — phylogenetic comparative methods, measured selection gradients, and field manipulation — and by about 1990 a functional claim with none of the three was unpublishable.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'Cardboard heads: the gull chick\'s releaser', who:'Niko Tinbergen & A. C. Perdeck, Terschelling', y:'1950', fig:'gullmodel',
   setup:'Newly hatched herring gull chicks, tested within a day or two of hatching, in the colony. The stimuli are flat cardboard models of an adult gull\'s head in profile, approximately life size, mounted on a wire. The experimenter moves a model past the chick in a standardised arc and counts the chick\'s pecks in a fixed interval; scores are expressed relative to a standard complete model presented in the same session. Models vary one feature at a time: the colour of the bill, the presence, colour and contrast of the spot at the gonys, the colour of the head, the shape of the head, and whether there is a head at all. The most-quoted stimulus in the series is not a gull head but a thin red rod carrying three white bars.',
   meas:'The spot near the tip of the bill accounts for most of the response: removing it roughly halves pecking. Changing the head colour makes little difference, and a bill presented without a head elicits nearly as much pecking as the full model. The red rod with white bars scored higher than any realistic gull head.',
   infer:'The chick is not responding to a gull. It is responding to an elongated, low, moving object with a contrasting spot near its end — a small feature set, some of whose members can be exaggerated beyond the natural range to give a stronger response. The first conclusion is a substantive claim about perception and has replicated. The second, the supernormal stimulus, is the one carrying the theoretical weight, and it is the weaker of the two.',
   assume:'That the chick\'s motivation is constant across a session, that presentation is equivalent across models, and that the experimenter\'s handling does not differ with the model. Carel ten Cate went back to the original protocols in 2009 and found that the models were presented by an experimenter who knew which was which, that presentation order was not randomised and was often fixed, so that declining motivation within a session is confounded with model order, and that the rod-and-bars result rests on small numbers. Hailman\'s later work adds a second problem: chicks\' pecking accuracy improves markedly with experience in the first days, so some of what was measured as an innate releasing mechanism is calibrated by feedback. The releaser conclusion survives; the founding demonstration of the supernormal stimulus does not carry the weight it is routinely given.'},
  {n:'The stickleback\'s red belly', who:'Niko Tinbergen, Leiden', y:'1936–1951', fig:null,
   setup:'Male three-spined sticklebacks are kept in individual tanks and brought into reproductive condition, at which point the throat and belly turn red and the male defends a nest territory. Two classes of model are presented on a wire in a standard position and moved by hand: an anatomically accurate plaster cast of a male stickleback with no red, and crude cigar-shaped models of wax or plasticine with a red underside. Response is scored as attacks or bites in a fixed period. A variant tests orientation, presenting red models tilted head-down, the posture a real male adopts in threat.',
   meas:'The crude red-bellied models were attacked far more than the accurate colourless cast. Head-down red models drew more attack than horizontal ones. Tinbergen also reported that captive males in a tank by a window displayed when a red Royal Mail van passed outside; this is an anecdote and should be recorded as one.',
   infer:'The releasing stimulus for territorial attack is "red underside on a moving object in the right orientation", not "conspecific male". Together with the gull work this establishes the general claim that animals respond to impoverished feature sets rather than to objects — which is the discipline\'s substantive contribution to the psychology of perception.',
   assume:'That reproductive and motivational state is constant, controlled by re-testing each fish against the standard model between trials. That model movement is standardised, which it is not: it was done by hand, and this is a real weakness of the entire dummy method. That bites are a ratio-scaled measure of response tendency, which they are not. Later replications, Rowland\'s among them, support the general finding but report smaller and more context-dependent effects than the classic account. The van story has never been tested, and it is a good illustration of how a striking observation can become a textbook datum without ever having been an experiment.'},
  {n:'Imprinting and the sensitive period', who:'Konrad Lorenz, Altenberg; Eckhard Hess, Chicago', y:'1935; 1959–1973', fig:'imprint',
   setup:'Lorenz\'s version is hand-rearing: greylag goslings hatched in an incubator, the first moving thing they see being Lorenz, who then walks and swims with them. Hess made it a measurement. Eggs are hatched in darkness so that the exact post-hatch age of each duckling is known to the hour. The apparatus is a circular runway about 1.5 m across with a decoy mallard mounted on a rotating arm, moving at a set speed and emitting a recorded call. A duckling of known age is released into the runway in a box and its following is recorded for a ten-minute exposure. After a delay the duckling is tested between the familiar decoy and a novel one, both in motion, and a preference score is computed. Hess also varied the effort required — adding a low hurdle, or a slope — to see whether the work done during exposure mattered.',
   meas:'The proportion of ducklings imprinting successfully, scored by later preference for the familiar decoy, peaked for exposure beginning at about 13–16 hours after hatching and fell off on both sides of that peak — ducklings exposed at four hours or at thirty hours did substantially worse than those exposed at fourteen. Effort expended during the exposure, measured as distance followed or obstacles surmounted, predicted the strength of the later preference better than the sheer duration of exposure did; Hess called this the law of effort.',
   infer:'A social preference is acquired within a bounded developmental window whose limits can be stated in hours rather than as a figure of speech, and the acquisition is not ordinary reinforcement learning: nothing rewards the duckling for following, and the harder it works the stronger the attachment, which is the reverse of what a reward account predicts. The inference that is not deductive is from a population curve to a mechanism in an individual: a falling proportion of successful imprinting with age is equally consistent with a window that closes and with a threshold that rises continuously.',
   assume:'That hatching time fixes the developmental clock, that the test preference measures what the exposure established, and that ducklings hatched and held in darkness are otherwise normal. The last is the problem. Hess\'s sharp peak has not replicated uniformly, and the boundaries of the period depend on rearing conditions: ducklings reared in light, or in company, show shifted or broadened periods. Bateson\'s work showed that the period closes partly because the animal develops a fear of novel objects, which is itself experience-dependent — so the "sensitive period" is not a clock but an interaction, and its boundaries are partly a function of the experiment that measures them. The irreversibility claim has been qualified: filial preferences can be substantially reversed with prolonged exposure, while sexual imprinting, demonstrated by Immelmann\'s cross-fostering of zebra finches onto Bengalese finches, is far more durable.'},
  {n:'Patch depletion in an aviary: testing the marginal value theorem', who:'Richard Cowie, in John Krebs\'s laboratory, Oxford (after Krebs, Ryan & Charnov 1974)', y:'1977', fig:'patchav',
   setup:'An indoor aviary is furnished with artificial trees: wooden posts bearing branches, each carrying plastic cups filled with sawdust in which a fixed number of mealworms is buried. Each cup is a depleting patch — the gain curve is measured directly, by recording the cumulative number of worms taken against time spent in the cup, and it is concave because worms get harder to find as they are removed. Travel time between patches is manipulated not by moving the trees but by fitting the cups with cardboard lids of two different tightnesses, so that the time to get into a patch is a few seconds in one treatment and of order twenty seconds in the other. Great tits are individually colour-ringed, given experience of the layout, and then observed through one-way glass with event recorders logging entry to and exit from each cup and the time of every capture. Krebs, Ryan and Charnov had run the parallel design with black-capped chickadees three years earlier, testing giving-up times rather than residence times.',
   meas:'Mean patch residence time was longer in the long-travel-time treatment, in the direction the theorem requires. The quantitative agreement with the predicted optimum held when the currency was taken to be *net* energy gain — that is, with the energetic cost of working the lids subtracted, measured separately — and did not hold for gross intake.',
   infer:'Birds leave patches approximately when the instantaneous rate of gain falls to the long-run average for the habitat, and the currency they behave as though maximising is net rather than gross energy. A flat relation between travel time and residence time, or a negative one, would have refuted the prediction outright.',
   assume:'That the gain curve is the same in both treatments — it is measured rather than assumed, but the bird\'s own search behaviour partly determines it, so g(t) is not an independent parameter in the way the derivation supposes. That the bird knows the habitat-wide average rate, which the theorem requires and which is a strong assumption about memory in an animal officially credited with no representations at all. And the one usually omitted from the textbook account: the travel cost was brought into the currency after the comparison against gross intake failed, so the celebrated quantitative fit is a fit with one adjusted term. Parker and Maynard Smith would say this is exactly what an optimality model is for — the failure located the currency. That is a fair defence and it should be stated alongside the result, not instead of it.'},
  {n:'Alarm calls and kinship in Belding\'s ground squirrels', who:'Paul Sherman, Tioga Pass, Sierra Nevada', y:'1977', fig:null,
   setup:'An eight-year study of a marked population of *Urocitellus beldingi* at about 3000 m. Every animal in the study area is individually marked with fur dye and numbered ear tags. Matrilines are reconstructed from trapping at natal burrows, from the timing of emergence of litters, and from years of observation; the species is convenient because females are philopatric and settle near their birth burrow while males disperse before breeding, so an adult female\'s kin are physically nearby and an adult male\'s are not. Observers in towers record every natural predator encounter — weasels, badgers, coyotes, hawks — noting which squirrels were above ground, which called, the caller\'s identity, who was within earshot and how each was related to the caller, and what happened to each animal afterwards. Nothing is manipulated; the design is entirely observational, with the kinship structure of the population doing the work an experimenter cannot do.',
   meas:'Alarm calls to terrestrial predators were given overwhelmingly by females with living relatives present — mothers, daughters, sisters, aunts — and much less often by males and by females without nearby kin, with the difference far larger than could be accounted for by how often each class was above ground. Callers were stalked and attacked by predators more often than non-callers present at the same encounters.',
   infer:'Calling is nepotistic: its distribution tracks kinship rather than group membership, reciprocation, or simple exposure. This discriminates directly against the two available alternatives — a "for the good of the colony" account, which predicts no sex or kinship asymmetry, and reciprocal altruism, which predicts calling to be directed at frequent associates regardless of relatedness.',
   assume:'That pedigree relatedness may be substituted for Hamilton\'s regression coefficient. Belding\'s ground squirrel litters are frequently multiply sired, so littermate relatedness lies somewhere between 0.25 and 0.5 and was not genotyped in 1977. That the cost of calling is captured by the increased rate of being stalked — a proxy for a fitness cost, not a fitness cost. And, crucially, that there is a benefit at all: the fitness gain to the warned relatives was never measured and, in this system, is very hard to measure, because it is a counterfactual about animals that were in fact warned. Sherman established that the behaviour is nepotistic, which is a genuine and non-trivial result. He did not test Hamilton\'s rule, which needs a b that nobody has obtained, and the textbooks that present this study as a quantitative confirmation of rb > c are misreporting it.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'Behaviour can be divided into discrete, countable units.', k:'definition', n:'The ethogram is a construction and different ethograms give different answers; the units are chosen by the observer, and inter-observer agreement on a behavioural category is respectable rather than high. Every quantitative claim in the field is relative to a categorisation nobody has independently validated.'},
  {a:'The behaviour observed is at or near an optimum.', k:'idealisation', n:'The methodological core of behavioural ecology. Held as a working assumption rather than a belief, and defended as such: a failed prediction is read as a misspecified currency or constraint set. This is a legitimate research strategy and it leaves the programme as a whole beyond the reach of observation, which its practitioners should say plainly and mostly do not.'},
  {a:'The currency being maximised is known.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Net energy per unit time, energetic efficiency, intake subject to a predation risk constraint and lifetime reproductive output give different optima, and the choice among them is usually made after seeing the data. Cowie\'s addition of travel cost to the currency is the honest published instance.'},
  {a:'Pedigree relatedness equals the regression coefficient in Hamilton\'s rule.', k:'idealisation', n:'Legitimate only under random mating with no genotypic assortment at the relevant loci, and therefore false in exactly the cases — viscous populations, kin discrimination, greenbeards — that make the theory interesting. Most field tests use it anyway, because the regression coefficient requires genotypes and social interaction data together.'},
  {a:'Fitness effects of social partners combine additively.', k:'idealisation', n:'Required for the Price-equation derivation of rb > c to have terms that mean what "cost" and "benefit" mean in ordinary usage. Where cooperation is synergistic, the partial regressions remain well defined and stop corresponding to anything a biologist recognises; the rule stays true and stops being informative.'},
  {a:'The population is at evolutionary equilibrium with its current environment.', k:'idealisation', n:'Much behaviour is measured in provisioned, captive or human-altered settings, and an optimality prediction concerns a strategy shaped by an environment that may no longer exist. Evolutionary mismatch is invoked freely as an explanation of failures and almost never tested independently.'},
  {a:'Behaviour in an aviary or laboratory is the same behaviour as in the field.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The trade that made behavioural ecology quantitative. Aviary birds have no predators, no competitors and no thermoregulatory costs, so the constraint set in the model is not the constraint set the animal evolved under. Where both have been done, laboratory and field estimates of the same parameter often differ by a factor of two or more.'},
  {a:'"Innate" names a coherent property of a trait.', k:'contested', n:'Lehrman\'s 1953 objection stands: an isolation experiment does not remove experience, only some experiences. Mameli and Bateson catalogue more than twenty distinct senses in current use, few of which entail the others, and the word continues to do rhetorical work in exactly the disputes where its ambiguity matters most.'},
  {a:'The observer\'s presence does not alter the behaviour recorded.', k:'empirical', n:'Testable and routinely tested by habituation periods, hides, one-way glass and remote recording. It usually holds for approach-tolerant species after days to weeks, and it demonstrably fails for others, which is a source of taxonomic bias in what the field knows about.'},
  {a:'Behavioural categories defined in one species transfer to another.', k:'contested', n:'"Aggression", "play", "deception" and "altruism" are applied across taxa on the strength of surface resemblance. Homology of behaviour requires the same evidence as homology of structure and is rarely offered, so comparative claims often rest on an analogy that has been lexically concealed.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`r`, r:'"r", relatedness',
   m:'In Hamilton\'s rule, the regression coefficient of the recipient\'s breeding value on the actor\'s — how much better than a random member of the population the recipient predicts the actor\'s genotype to be. It equals the pedigree fraction only under random mating with no assortment. Note the collision with r as a vessel radius on the physiology sheet and with r as an intrinsic rate of increase in ecology.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`b,\ c`, r:'"benefit" and "cost"',
   m:'Partial derivatives of fitness with respect to the social act, holding everything else fixed — not gross gains and losses. Both are defined relative to a specified statistical model, and both are far harder to measure than r, which is why almost every field test of rb > c measures r well, c badly and b not at all.', d:'offspring, or fitness units'},
  {s:R`W_{\rm incl}=W_{\rm direct}+\sum_j r_j\,\Delta W_j`, r:'"inclusive fitness equals direct fitness plus sum over j of r-j delta-W-j"',
   m:'The actor\'s effect on the propagation of its own alleles, summed over all bearers and weighted by relatedness. The ΔW is essential and routinely dropped in popular statements: only the *change* the actor causes is counted, never the total fitness of its relatives, which is what stops the quantity double-counting.', d:'offspring equivalents'},
  {s:R`\Delta\bar z=\frac{\mathrm{Cov}(w_i,z_i)}{\bar w}+\frac{\mathrm{E}(w_i\Delta z_i)}{\bar w}`, r:'"delta z-bar", the Price equation',
   m:'An exact identity for the change in the mean of any character z across one generation: a selection term plus a transmission term. w_i is individual i\'s absolute fitness, z_i its character value, and the bars are population means. Its virtue is that it assumes nothing; its limitation is the same thing, since an identity organises rather than predicts.', d:'z in trait units; w in offspring'},
  {s:R`z_i,\ \bar z`, r:'"z-i", "z-bar"',
   m:'The character value of individual i and the population mean. In social-evolution derivations z is a breeding value rather than a phenotype, which is a substitution that carries an additivity assumption silently: only for an additive genetic architecture does the regression of phenotype on genotype support the interpretation the rule needs.', d:'trait units'},
  {s:R`g(t),\ \tau,\ t^{*}`, r:'"g of t", "tau", "t-star"',
   m:'The cumulative gain from a patch after t in it, the travel time between patches, and the optimal residence time. g must be concave for the theorem to have an interior solution — an empirical claim about how patches deplete, and one that fails for patches that improve with handling.', d:'g in J or items; τ and t in s'},
  {s:R`\left.\frac{\partial g}{\partial t}\right|_{t^*}=\frac{g(t^*)}{\tau+t^*}=E^{*}`, r:'"marginal value theorem"',
   m:'Leave when the instantaneous rate falls to the long-run average E*. The graphical form draws a tangent to g from −τ on the time axis. What the notation hides is that E* is a property of the whole habitat, so the rule requires the forager to have information it is nowhere credited with having.', d:'rate: J s⁻¹ or items s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`E(S,T)`, r:'"E of S against T", the payoff to S when played against T',
   m:'The payoff function in evolutionary game theory. Note the collision: E here is a payoff, while E in the Price equation two entries above is an expectation. S is evolutionarily stable if E(S,S) > E(T,S) for every mutant T, or E(S,S) = E(T,S) together with E(S,T) > E(T,T) — the second clause being what distinguishes an ESS from a Nash equilibrium.', d:'fitness units'},
  {s:R`p^{*}(\text{Hawk})=V/C`, r:'"p-star equals V over C"',
   m:'The stable frequency of the escalating strategy when the cost of injury exceeds the value of the resource. V is the resource value and C the cost of losing an escalated fight, both in fitness units — and C here collides with C as the constraint set in the optimality formulation, which the literature tolerates. The model cannot distinguish a polymorphic population from a randomising individual.', d:'p dimensionless; V, C in fitness units'},
  {s:R`r^{*}=\frac{n-1}{2n}`, r:'"r-star equals n minus one over two n"',
   m:'The optimal proportion of sons under local mate competition with n foundresses per patch. Yet another r: this one is a sex ratio, unrelated to relatedness. With one foundress it is near zero, with two 0.25, and it approaches Fisher\'s 1/2 as n grows. A parameter-free quantitative prediction, which is rare in this field.', d:'dimensionless (proportion male)'},
  {s:R`h^{2}=\frac{V_A}{V_P}`, r:'"h-squared", narrow-sense heritability',
   m:'The ratio of additive genetic to total phenotypic variance in a particular population in a particular environment. For behavioural traits typically 0.2–0.5, lower than for morphology. It is a variance ratio and says nothing about any individual, and nothing about whether a trait is modifiable — a point that has to be made afresh in every generation.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\arg\max_{s\in S}W(s)\ \text{s.t. }C`, r:'"arg max over s in S of W of s, subject to C"',
   m:'The optimality schema: S is the strategy set, W the currency, C the constraint set. Everything contestable in behavioural ecology lives in S and C rather than in the maximisation, because S fixes what alternatives the animal is being compared against and those alternatives do not exist. Choosing S is the modelling step that Gould and Lewontin objected to and that no amount of data constrains.', d:'W in fitness units; S, C are sets'}
 ],
 worked:{
  t:'Does the alarm call pay? Hamilton\'s rule with the numbers we actually have',
  steps:[
   'Set up the rule: the call spreads if rb > c, with r the relatedness of the caller to those warned, b the fitness benefit to them, c the fitness cost to the caller — all in the same units, taken here as expected offspring.',
   'r, measured: a female Belding\'s ground squirrel\'s nearby kin are her mother, daughters and littermate sisters. Pedigree values are 0.5, but litters are frequently multiply sired, so littermates are a mixture of full and half sibs. Take a mean r to a randomly chosen nearby relative of about 0.35. This term is the one the field data genuinely constrain.',
   'c, measured by proxy: Sherman recorded that callers were stalked and attacked more often than non-callers at the same encounters — of order twice as often. Suppose a non-caller\'s probability of being killed in a given encounter is 0.01 and a caller\'s is 0.02, so the excess is 0.01. With a mean residual reproductive value of about two future offspring, c ≈ 0.01 × 2 = 0.02 offspring.',
   'b, not measured: this is the term nobody has. Take, illustratively, 3.5 relatives within earshot, each of whose per-encounter death probability is cut from 0.01 to 0.007 by the warning — a 30% reduction, chosen because it is plausible and for no other reason. Then b = 3.5 × 0.003 × 2 = 0.021 offspring.',
   'Evaluate: rb = 0.35 × 0.021 = 0.0074, against c = 0.02. On these numbers rb < c by a factor of about 2.7, and the call does not pay.',
   'Now find what would make it pay. Holding r and c fixed, we need b ≈ 0.057 — so either the call must cut each relative\'s risk by about 80% rather than 30%, or there must be about nine relatives in earshot rather than three and a half. Both are entirely possible and neither has been measured.'],
  a:'The rule fails by a factor of about three on one set of plausible assumptions and succeeds on another equally plausible set, and nothing in eight years of field data decides between them, because b is a counterfactual — what would have become of the relatives had no call been given — in a system where the call cannot be withheld. Two conclusions, and they point in opposite directions. Sherman\'s study is a real result: the distribution of calls tracks kinship in a way that group-benefit and reciprocity accounts do not predict, and it is the distribution, not the arithmetic, that carries the argument. But the textbook presentation of it as a quantitative confirmation of rb > c is false, and the difficulty is structural rather than a matter of insufficient effort. Hamilton\'s rule is close to a theorem given its own definitions and close to untestable given the difficulty of measuring its terms, and the ground squirrels are where you can watch that tension in the field.'}
}

};
